    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nRemoving a window type from `'sessionoptions'` to avoid saving its contents also discards the saved sizes of every window in that tabpage.\n\nA window excluded by `'sessionoptions'` clears `restore_size` for the whole tabpage, and `ses_winsizes()` then writes `wincmd =` in place of per-window sizes:\n\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/40740f49f839cb81fb5c70eeceedcaca0a0568ab/src/nvim/ex_session.c#L826-L831\n\nSame shape as #37330, fixed for floating windows in #37344.\n\nNOTE: I'm not sure if this is part of the contract of 'sessionoptions', even\nafter reading the docs. For example, excluding 'terminal' should remove\nintelligent restore behavior from it, but should it *also* corrupt the layout of\nall other windows that are not a terminal in the process?\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nnvim --clean\n:set sessionoptions-=terminal\n:edit /tmp/a | vsplit /tmp/b | vertical resize 30\n:botright vsplit | terminal\n:mksession! /tmp/S.vim\n```\n\n`/tmp/S.vim` contains `wincmd =` and no `resize` commands. Leave `terminal` in `'sessionoptions'` and it contains `exe 'vert 1resize ' . ...` as expected.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe two file windows keep their widths.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1367+g40740f49f8\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nYes, Vim 9.1 writes `wincmd =` - same code path.\n",
